ively pre-flop.\n\n**Position**: This appears to be the pre-flop stage with no public cards shown. The action options (including check) suggest I'm either in the big blind facing no raise, or both players checked.\n\n**Stack Situation**: I have 19,950 chips (nearly full stack) and am ahead by 109 cumulative chips over 7 hands. This is early in the match (hand 8 of 20), so I have plenty of room to build pots with strong hands.\n\n**Strategic Options**:\n1. **Check (0)**: Passive play that gives free card - wastes the value of my premium hand\n2. **Raise 200**: Small raise (2x BB assuming 100 BB) - builds pot but might not get enough value\n3. **Larger Raise (400-600)**: Standard 3-4x BB raise - appropriate for hand strength\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Too aggressive pre-flop with deep stacks; only justified against very tight opponents\n5. **Fold (-1)**: Terrible with A-Q in heads-up\n\nA-Q plays well post-flop and dominates many hands my opponent might call with (A-J, A-10, K-Q, etc.). I should raise to build the pot and take initiative.\n\nReason:\nA-Q offsuit is strong enough in heads-up to raise for value. A standard raise of 400 chips (4x BB) accomplishes multiple goals: it builds the pot with my premium hand, allows me to maintain a reasonable stack-to-pot ratio for post-flop play, and puts pressure on weaker hands. This sizing is large enough to charge draws and weak hands but not so large that I only get action from hands that dominate me.\n\nAnswer: 400"}
