s, Q of Diamonds, 10 of Clubs, 8 of Hearts.\n\n10: High Card: No made hand; highest single card wins. \n\nNote for Action representation: -1 for Fold, -2 for All-in. 0 for Call/Check. >0 for the amount you raise the bet. \n\nWhen placing a raise, the chips bet must be at least twice the current maximum bet of this round. If no player has bet before in this round, the raise must be at least equal to the big blind.\n\nTo call or raise, a player must have enough chips to cover the required bet. Otherwise, they can only fold or go all-in. An improper bet is considered an illegal action and will be treated as a fold.\n\nNow is your turn, to your best ability, please select an action from your valid actions that maximize your probability of winning. If you have more chips than your opponent by the last hand of the game, you win the game.\nPlease briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output the action with specified format.\n    Your response format should be:\n    Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n    Reason: [Your Reason]\n    Answer: [Your Answer] (should be a number)\n", 'user_prompt': 'This is the 20 hand out of 20 hands. Everyone starts with 20,000 chips each hand, and you have 19950. Your cumulative win/loss on chips until last hand are -9572.0, but the cumulative win/loss chips are inaccessible and independent from your current chips (consider them as deposits/debts to a bank).You are currently losing to your opponent.\nYour cards are: 3 of Diamonds, King of Clubs.\nCurrently there is no public card shown.\nThe Game history is as follows: \nYour valid actions are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 200 (raise the bet), or any value above 200 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:\nLet me analyze this critical final hand situation:\n\n**Current Position:**\n- This is hand 20/20 (final hand)\n- I have 19,950 chips vs opponent's 20,050 chips\n- I'm down 100 chips this hand\n- Cumulative I'm losing by 9,572 chips overall\n- My hole cards: K♦3♦ (King-high with weak kicker)\n\n**Hand Strength:**\n- K-3 offsuit is a weak hand (bottom 30% of starting hands)\n- Pre-flop, this is typically a fold in most positions\n- However, in heads-up play, hand values shift - any King has some showdown value\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n1. **This is the final hand** - I need to win THIS hand to overcome my chip deficit\n2. **Chip counts are close** - I'm only 100 chips behind, so any significant pot win gives me the match\n3. **Aggression value** - In heads-up, checking/calling allows opponent to dictate action\n4. **All-in pressure** - Going all-in puts maximum pressure on opponent to have a strong hand\n5. **Fold equity** - Opponent will fold many hands to an all-in, giving me immediate wins\n\n**Options Analysis:**\n- **Fold (-1)**: Guarantees I lose the match. Unacceptable.\n- **Check (0)**: Passive, allows opponent to control. Risky with weak hand.\n- **Small raise (200)**: Shows weakness, commits chips without fold equity\n- **All-in (-2)**: Maximum pressure, fold equity, chance to win outright or see all cards\n\n**Key Insight:** Since this is the FINAL hand and I'm behind, I must take calculated risks. An all-in maximizes my fold equity and puts maximum pressure on my opponent. If they fold, I win the match instantly.\n\nReason: \nThis is the last hand and I'm currently losing the match. With K-3, I have a weak but not worthless hand in heads-up play. Going all-in provides maximum fold equity and pressure - my opponent must have a strong hand or strong read to call with 20,000 chips at risk. If called, I still have outs with my King. Passive play or small raises won't generate enough pressure to overcome my deficit. This is a calculated high-risk, high-reward play necessary in this must-win situation.\n\nAnswer: -2"}
